---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const kids = [
  ["John Augustus II", "1847–1935", "Burgersdorp", "m. Alida Jacoba van Ellinckhuyzen, 1870, DRC Colesberg"],
  ["James Henry", "1848–1939", "Burgersdorp", "m. Maria Margaretha Hester Catherina Louw, 1867, Dordrecht; died at Manor Owen aged 92"],
  ["George Augustus", "1850–1933", "Burgersdorp", "—"],
  ["Margaretha Louisa Maria (Bedford)", "1851–1915", "Burgersdorp", "m. Thomas Bedford, 1875, Colesberg"],
  ["Catherina Maria Sophia", "1855–1859", "Burgersdorp", "died aged four, 30 July 1859"],
  ["Hendrik Nicholaas", "1857–1931", "Dordrecht", "m. Anna Johanna Maria Schoemann, 1877, Barkly East"],
  ["Catherine Maria Sophia “Kathleen”", "1859–1940", "Barkly East", "m. Willem Hermanus Booyzen, 1879 — the line to David"],
  ["Willem Frederick Thomas “Tom”", "1861–1890", "Barkly East", "m. Hendrina Petronella Jacomina Dry, 1886; died at 29"],
  ["Frederick Fortunatus", "1863–1952", "Burgersdorp", "the forename reappears as Frederik Fortsenatus Booÿsen, 1899"],
  ["Richard Peter", "1866–1946", "Lady Grey", "—"],
  ["— (a duplicate)", "—", "—", "the tree also lists a “Kathleen Mary Barry (Booysen)”. The 1915 death notice shows she is the same woman as Kathleen above. Withdrawn."],
];
---
<Base title="The Barrys"
      eyebrow="An Irishman born at sea, or in Yorkshire, or in Cork"
      dek="The Barrys gave this family its English forenames, its farm at Manor Owen, its politics — and its single most contradictory record. Everything from 1819 forward is solid. Everything before it falls apart.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>John Augustus Barry I, 1819–1903</h2>
  <p class="dek">The most documented person in the Booyzen ancestry, and the least certain.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:12rem">Born</td><td>8 June 1819 — recorded in the tree, literally, as
          <strong class="m">"?Sunderland, Durham, England? Ireland? High Seas?"</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trades</td><td><strong>Carpenter and Builder</strong> · <em>timmerman en bouwvakker</em> ·
          <strong>Grocer</strong> · <strong>Labourer</strong> — five occupation records, all working trades</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Married</td><td>c. 23 February 1846, <strong>Dutch Reformed Church, Colesberg</strong> —
          Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Mary <strong>Kolbe</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Died</td><td><strong>3 August 1903</strong> on the farm <strong>Manorowen</strong>,
          Barkly East — <em>the tree gives 25 August; his 1915 death notice gives the 3rd.</em>
          His son James Henry died there too, in 1939, aged 92</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The 1915 death notice is now in hand</h2>
  <p class="dek">The tree carried a long research note describing three conflicting documents. One of the
    three — <b>the 1915 death notice, typewritten and therefore unarguable</b> — turned out to be sitting
    in the tree's own photo store, unread. It can now be quoted rather than paraphrased.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1915-john-barry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Typewritten 1915 death notice for John Barry of Barkly East." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of John Barry, prepared at Barkly East and signed by his second
      son <strong>James H. Barry</strong>, 29 March 1915. <a href={u("/documents/")}>Full transcription &rarr;</a></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:15rem"><strong>Birthplace and nationality</strong></td>
            <td><strong>"At sea (exact whereabouts unknown), British."</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Father</td><td>John Barry (address unknown)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mother</strong></td><td><strong>Barry, born Neagle</strong> — Christian names and address unknown</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Occupation</td><td>Carpenter and builder</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td><strong>Manorowen</strong>, Barkly East District — and he owned <strong>no immovable property</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place of last marriage</td><td><strong>"Burghersdorp (probably)"</strong> — the informant was not sure</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>"Manorowen" is one word, and it is Welsh.</b> The tree renders the farm "Manor Owen", which hides
    it. <b>Manorowen is a village in Pembrokeshire.</b> A Welsh farm-name on a supposedly Irish family's
    holding in the north-eastern Cape is a completely unexplored thread — and he did not own the place.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Three answers to one question</h2>
  <p>An extended research note carried in the tree — written by an earlier researcher, not by this archive —
    sets out why nobody knows where he was born. The 1915 document above is the second of the three.</p>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">Death certificate, 1903</div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:6px">"An Irishman born in Ireland"</h3>
      <p class="cap">Recorded by the <strong>Cape Mounted Rifles</strong>, then the authority in the
        district, stationed at <strong>Johnson's Leap</strong> — and dated two months after his death.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">Document, 29 March 1915</div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:6px">"At sea, exact whereabouts unknown. British."</h3>
      <p class="cap">Signed by his second son <strong>James Henry Barry</strong>. It also names his father
        as <strong>John Barry</strong> and his mother's maiden name as <strong>"Neagle"</strong> — almost
        certainly <strong>Nagle</strong>, of County Cork.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">The tree's own guess</div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:6px">Sunderland, Durham</h3>
      <p class="cap">Offered with a question mark and no source. The note's own conclusion is a question:
        if he was born on the high seas with the <strong>1820 Settlers</strong>, who were his parents?</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The 1820 Settler hypothesis does not survive arithmetic.</b> He was born <b>8 June 1819</b>. The
    settler fleets sailed from late 1819 and reached Algoa Bay from April 1820. He cannot have been born on
    that voyage. <b>But the family does have a documented 1820 Settler</b> — his own father-in-law,
    <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b>, who sailed on the <em>Nautilus</em> from Gravesend on 3 December 1819.
    <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> The likeliest reading is that a true settler story attached
    itself to the wrong branch. <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The line that makes the rebel story make sense</h2>
  <blockquote>
    "As an Irishman, John Barry was reputed to be <strong>anti English</strong>, and as his son and grandson
    were married to Dutch girls <strong>their activity during the Boer War would be interesting</strong>."
  </blockquote>
  <p class="cap">From the same research note in the tree. Written by someone else, about their own family,
    long before this archive existed.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p>It lands exactly on the Booyzen name-change testimony. His daughter <b>Kathleen Barry married Willem
      Hermanus Booyzen</b> — the man recorded as changing <em>Booysen</em> to <em>Booyzen</em> so as not to
      be taken for a British-siding joiner. An <b>anti-English Irish grandfather at Barkly East</b>, whose
      children married almost entirely into Cape Dutch families, is precisely the household in which
      rebel sympathies would be unremarkable.</p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> This is the most testable inference in the archive, and it is
      still an inference. Nobody has yet found a Barry or a Booysen on a rebel roll.
      <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>He married into Afrikanerdom, not the other way round</h2>
  <p>An English-speaking carpenter of Irish reputation married a Cape-born woman <b>in the Dutch Reformed
    Church at Colesberg</b>, and within one generation his children had married
    <b>Louw</b>, <b>van Ellinckhuyzen</b>, <b>Schoemann</b>, <b>Dry</b>, <b>Bedford</b> — and
    <b>Booyzen</b>.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:16rem">Child</th><th>Dates</th><th>Born</th><th>Married</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{kids.map(([n, d, b, m]) => (
        <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="m">{d}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td>{m}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    Read rows five and seven together. <b>Catherina Maria Sophia Barry died on 30 July 1859.</b>
    <b>Catherine Maria Sophia Barry was born on 4 August 1859 — five days later</b>, and was given the dead
    child's name. That name then travelled: to <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen (1891–1946)</b>, and to
    <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen (1929–2013)</b>, David Defranceski's grandmother. A four-year-old's
    death in Burgersdorp is still being commemorated, unknowingly, four generations later.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>One sister fewer than the tree thinks</h2>
  <p>The tree lists <b>Catherine Maria Sophia "Kathleen" Barry (1859–1940), married Booyzen</b> and,
    separately, a sister <b>Kathleen Mary Barry (Booysen)</b> with no dates — implying two Barry sisters
    married two Booysens.</p>
  <p>The 1915 death notice lists <b>only one</b> Barry daughter married to a Boyson:
    <em>"Kathleen Mary Boyson, born Barry, married in community of property to William Boyson."</em>
    And the arithmetic closes exactly — the tree's <b>11</b> children equal the notice's <b>8</b> surviving,
    plus the <b>2</b> who predeceased him (Catherina Maria Sophia, d. 1859; Willem Frederick Thomas,
    d. 1890), plus <b>1 duplicate</b>.</p>
  <blockquote>
    <span class="chip doc">Resolved</span> They are the same woman. This archive's earlier claim of
    <strong>"two Barry sisters married Booysens" is withdrawn.</strong>
  </blockquote>
  <p class="cap">The third Barry–Booyzen tie stands: <b>Dorothea Regina Barry married Henry James
    Booyzen</b> at Elliot in 1912. <a href={u("/cousins/")}>The cousin marriage &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Kathleen Barry, 1859–1940</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Born at <b>Barkly East</b>, christened at <b>Dordrecht</b> in September 1859. Her occupation is
      recorded — rarely, and valuably — as <b>"Needle Woman"</b>. She married Willem Hermanus Booyzen on
      <b>22 December 1879</b> and bore <b>thirteen children</b>, of whom nine are dated.</p>
    <p>She outlived her husband by thirty-five years and <b>died on 5 September 1940 at No. 1, Fifteenth
      Avenue, Mayfair, Johannesburg</b>, of <b>carcinoma of the uterus</b> after thirteen months' illness.
      She was buried at <b>Brixton Cemetery</b>. She made the journey the testimony describes, and lived
      four decades on the Rand at the end of it.</p>
    <p>Her death certificate records her occupation as <b>Home Duties</b> — and, on the line below,
      <b>"Military Pensioner."</b> Her husband had been dead thirty-five years and died of an ulcer, so
      the pension is not his.</p>
    <p><span class="chip lore">Searched</span> The archive guessed a son killed in the First World War.
      <b>The war-dead registers say otherwise</b>: no Booyzen among the 1914–18 dead, no Booysen matching
      a son's forename, and <b>all her identifiable sons survived</b> — dying in 1932, 1952, 1956, 1966
      and 1980. The pension belongs to a man who <b>served and came home</b>.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Where it goes next &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>Nine years after her death, her granddaughter and namesake <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen married
      at St Joseph's Catholic Church, Mayfair</b>. <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>How far the Barry names travel</h2>
  <p class="dek">There is no Barry surname left in this family after 1859. There is a great deal of
    Barry <em>name</em>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1942-eleven-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Eleven Daschner children listed on a 1942 death notice, several carrying Barry and Booyzen forenames." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The children of <strong>Catharina Maria Sophia Booysen</strong> &mdash;
      Kathleen Barry's daughter &mdash; on her husband's 1942 death notice.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Kathleen Barry married a Booyzen in the 1880s, so the surname stops with her. Her daughter married
      a Daschner, so it is two surnames gone in two generations. Then look at what those eleven
      grandchildren are called:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Catherine Mary Sophia</b> Daschner &mdash; Kathleen's own name, third generation running</li>
      <li><b>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth</b> Jeanette Daschner &mdash; the Barry name that also runs
        down the Pretorius side of this family and reaches
        <a href={u("/who/margaretha-wilhelmina-elizabeth-maggie-booyzen/")}>Maggie Booyzen</a> in 1926</li>
      <li><b>Richard Peter</b> Daschner &mdash; for Kathleen's brother
        <a href={u("/who/richard-peter-barry/")}>Richard Peter Barry</a> of Manorowen</li>
      <li><b>Henry James</b> Daschner &mdash; for her brother
        <a href={u("/who/henry-james-booyzen/")}>Henry James Booyzen</a></li>
      <li><b>William Herman</b> Daschner &mdash; for her father Willem Hermanus</li>
    </ul>
    <blockquote><b>Five of eleven.</b> The Barrys stop being a surname in this archive in 1859 and go on
      being a set of first names for another eighty years.</blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">And a sixth, off the 1942 list</span> The Johannesburg cemetery index
      carries a <b>George Downing Daschner</b>, buried at Brixton on 20 June 1925, the year he was born.
      He is not on his father's death notice because he was dead seventeen years before it was written
      &mdash; a twelfth child. <b>George Downing</b> is the name of Kathleen Barry's own grandson, born
      in 1901, and here it is on an infant in another family in the same decade. A second of the name
      died in 1998.</p>
    <p>This comes from an index and not from an image, and the page says so. But it moves the reading:
      <b>six names, not five</b>, and a pattern that cannot comfortably be called coincidence.
      <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>The namesakes &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>This matters to the rebel question in a small, real way. When the Cape Rebel roll produces a
      <b>Fred J. Barry</b> of Ivanhoe and a <b>Richard Peter Barry</b> of Jerpoint, the second name is
      not a coincidence to be waved at &mdash; it is a name this family demonstrably used and kept using.
      It still does not prove the man on the roll is Kathleen's brother, and this archive still marks
      that <span class="chip open">unproven</span>. But it does mean the name is evidence of a family,
      not of an individual. <a href={u("/documents/#daschner-1942")}>The 1942 notice &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="index-1834-1912">Not one of them is in the Master's index</h2>
  <p class="dek">Film <span class="m">007844477</span> is the Cape Master's own
    <em>Index of Estates 1834&ndash;1912</em> &mdash; every estate reported in the Colony for
    seventy-eight years, alphabetically, with the death-notice number beside each name. <b>Barry runs
    across three pages and this family is on none of them.</b></p>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/index-estates-barry-p232.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Page 232 of the Cape Master's Index of Estates: Barry, A to H." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>232</strong> &mdash; A to H. Two Fredericks, <em>Johannes</em>
        and <em>William</em>. <b>No Fortunatus.</b></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/index-estates-barry-p233.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Page 233 of the Cape Master's Index of Estates: Barry, H to J." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>233</strong> &mdash; the Johns. Five plain, then Charles, Daniel,
        Henry, Henry, Joseph, William. <b>No John Augustus.</b></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/index-estates-barry-p234.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Page 234 of the Cape Master's Index of Estates: Barry, M to W, then Barsby." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>234</strong> &mdash; M to W, then Barsby. Two Richards, 1836 and
        1858. <b>No Richard Peter, and no Margaretha at all.</b></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The archive went to this index for one thing: to settle whether the
      <a href={u("/rebels/")}>Cape Rebel</a> <b>Richard Peter Barry of Jerpoint</b> is Kathleen's
      brother, by finding his estate. He is not there. Neither is
      <b>Frederick Fortunatus</b>, nor <b>Dorothea Regina</b>, nor <b>Hendrik Nicholaas</b>, nor
      <b>Catherine Maria Sophia</b>, nor <b>Kathleen Mary</b>.</p>
    <p>And neither, more startlingly, is <b>John Augustus Barry</b>, who died in August 1903 in the
      middle of the index's range, or his widow <b>Margaretha Kolbe</b>, who died in 1912, the year it
      closes.</p>
    <blockquote><b>Then the reason arrives, and it is not an absence at all.</b> This archive has
      already read their death notices. Both are dated <b>29 March 1915</b>, sworn by their sons.
      <b>The index stops in 1912. They filed in 1915.</b></blockquote>
    <p>John Augustus Barry died in August 1903 and nobody reported his estate for
      <b>twelve years</b>. That is not carelessness with a form. It is the third instance of the same
      thing in this archive:</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th class="m">Died</th><th class="m">Reported</th><th class="m">Gap</th><th>Who filed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>John Augustus Barry</b> and <b>Margaretha Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">1903 · 1912</td><td class="m">29 Mar 1915</td><td class="m"><b>12 years</b></td><td>two sons, together</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href={u("/who/isabella-frederika-coetsee/")}>Isabella Fredrika Booyzen, born Coetzee</a></td><td class="m">31 Mar 1930</td><td class="m">1 Apr 1943</td><td class="m"><b>13 years</b></td><td>her husband</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href={u("/who/maria-louisa-bertha-kumm/")}>Maria Louisa Bertha Mountjoy, born Kumm</a></td><td class="m">3 Jul 1934</td><td class="m">19 Feb 1938</td><td class="m"><b>3&frac12; years</b></td><td>her son &mdash; "death not reported"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:18px">Three families, three decades, one habit. Twice a husband's estate and a
      wife's were filed <b>on the same day</b>, years after the fact, by a son.
      <b>The Master's office was something this family went to when it had to, not when somebody
      died</b> &mdash; and any search that assumes an estate was reported in the year of the death will
      miss them all. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Two of this archive's own corrections are exactly
      that mistake &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>And then it breaks</h2>
  <p>Above John Augustus Barry I the record becomes impossible. His recorded father,
    <b>John Barry (1796–1853)</b>, is <b>buried in Yorkshire five days before he dies in the Eastern
    Cape</b>. His parents are entered as "Unknown Barry" and "Unknown Barry"; a brother as
    "Other Barry's - South Africa Barry".</p>
  <p><b>The Barry line is documented from 1819 forward and is a knot before it.</b> The archive draws it as
    a break, not a chain. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>See the full contradiction &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
</Base>
